RELATIVE TO DESIGN O: the recursion acts on variant outcomes inside acquisition, about 400 times more meta-level data per CPU-second (on training problems, not fresh instances), instead of on outer discovery values that are mostly noise. Discovery knowledge travels between categories through the kernel's existing memory channel. The held-out test is therefore a plain kernel measurement, with no discovery on held-out categories and exact pairing (identical untrained answers).

RELATIVE TO DESIGN Q: q's inner prior w is a constant of the mechanism that only outer discovery can change, and outer discovery accepts almost nothing. Here the learner sets w itself from its own discoveries, and the test separates the on-policy (recursive) component from accumulation.

RELATIVE TO KNOWN ALGORITHMS: this is adaptive operator selection with credit assignment (probability matching over mutation operators), carried across problems. That is a familiar idea in evolutionary computation and hyper-heuristics, and close in spirit to the Owner's reference on predicting a modification's effect before making it, reduced here to a per-cell predictor. The update rule is human-written and conventional. It is not a radically new principle, and none is claimed.

WHAT IS NEW is the test:
- an on-policy versus off-policy contrast that isolates recursion from accumulation;
- a label-shuffled placebo;
- a ceiling arm that turns a null result into a named limit;
- chain-level replication of the meta-learning;
- a decisive region (at least 0.007) fixed by a power analysis before the run.
